From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:29:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] build: use tsrs with shared incremental build caches

Ship pinned tsrs with the CLI and use it for every configured application
check. Resolve its launcher relative to the installed SDK, including bundled
CommonJS and nested package-manager layouts. Remove compiler selection and
the obsolete project-local TypeScript version probe.

Force no emission for application checks, preserve watched-file observation
and concurrent-edit retries, and await diagnostic pipe closure. Keep parsed
diagnostics unformatted and decode subprocess UTF-8 across pipe chunks.
Accept an empty project only when its no-inputs diagnostic is the sole
reported error. Preserve enable, try and disable modes and check explicit
projects without bundling their application.

Use tsrs for SDK source checking and declaration emission. Generate public
and internal declarations once each, then copy the identical declarations
to their ESM and CommonJS package layouts. Keep package markers, declaration
maps and internal-API removal intact; esbuild still emits runtime JavaScript.
Describe ordinary buffer ownership without adding upload copies or requiring
generic typed-array syntax in public declarations.

Run SDK typechecks and declaration builds through one incremental runner.
Store build info and declarations together in atomic archives under the
shared project cache, keyed by compiler, platform, configuration, dependency
locks and build variant. Restore into independent staging directories so
compatible checkouts share state without mixing public and internal outputs.
Keep failed and custom compiler runs out of shared publication, remove
declarations for deleted sources, and treat concurrent eviction as a miss.

Provide completed-entry cleanup with configurable publication-age retention
while preserving temporary files, directories and unknown data. Document
cache ownership and controls, and run the real-compiler cache suites
separately from Vitest in CI.

Older tsc-based clients remain backend-compatible because checking does not
change deployment requests or emitted application JavaScript.
